     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order, even with your confirmation, because confirming it doesn't fix either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still five times the maximum.** Sertraline tops out at 200 mg per day for adults, and 1000 mg could seriously harm the patient. A dose that far over the limit would need a documented clinical reason from the treating prescriber, and in practice a pharmacist would challenge it too.\n- **You've said you're not a clinician.** Your confirmation can't take the place of a licensed prescriber's order, whatever your role in the hospital.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- **If this patient needs sertraline:** ask the attending physician on the medicine service to place the order with the dose they intend. They'll probably start at 25\u201350 mg daily.\n- **If you're testing the system's safety checks:** the pharmacy informatics or clinical informatics team can set this up in a test environment, and I can help you plan those test cases.\n- **If something unusual is going on with this patient,** such as concern about an intentional overdose: contact the attending physician or the on-call pharmacist right away.\n\nI can enter the order once a prescriber confirms a dose in the normal range."
      }
     ],
